{"id":"n-20261011-18i744","category":"sports","display_title":"Protest and Triumph at Il Lombardia","display_title_he":"מחאה וניצחון היסטורי בג'ירו די לומברדיה","short_summary":"Cyclists at the Giro di Lombardia staged a pre-race protest over rider safety before 20-year-old Paul Seixas secured a historic solo victory.","english_level":"C1","words":468,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"BBC Sport","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/cycling/articles/cqx2vm911870o","title":"'We lost the life of a brother' - rider protest delays Giro di Lombardia","published_at":"2026-10-10T11:57:40.000Z"},{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/10/giro-di-lombardia-cycling-safety-protest-evenepoel","title":"Seixas makes Giro di Lombardia history after riders stage safety protest","published_at":"2026-10-10T15:33:14.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-10T11:57:40.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-11T06:03:06.265Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The autumn classic known as the Giro di Lombardia—traditionally dubbed \"The Race of the Falling Leaves\"—commenced under unprecedented circumstances in Bergamo, Italy, when competitors staged a coordinated protest. When race officials completed the countdown and waved the initial starting flag, the peloton remained stationary, causing confusion among spectators. The riders delayed the 239-kilometre race by five minutes to draw urgent attention to safety failures and recurring collisions involving non-race vehicles. Addressing the crowd, double Olympic champion Remco Evenepoel highlighted the tragic death of 19-year-old British cyclist Finlay Tarling, who died in August after colliding with a vehicle at the Volta a Portugal. Evenepoel stressed that cyclists could no longer accept getting injured or dying while pursuing their sport.","The protest was further fueled by an incident earlier that week at Tre Valli Varesine, where riders Thomas Gloag and Marcel Camprubi were struck by a police motorcycle travelling in the opposite direction. The CPA, the professional cyclists' union, issued a statement re-posted on social media by stars including Evenepoel and Tom Pidcock. The CPA asserted that riders compete at extreme speeds with minimal protection, depending entirely on organizers and cycling's governing body, the UCI, to secure race routes. Striking a poignant tone, the statement declared that while athletes respect organizers and want to put on a show, their paramount priority is returning home safely to loved ones. The protest carried extra resonance for Evenepoel, who survived a life-threatening descent crash in this race in 2020.","When a second starting flag was waved, the peloton set off toward Como. Five-time defending champion Tadej Pogacar was absent following a season-ending Vuelta a España injury, leaving the field wide open. The decisive moment arrived on the Civiglio climb with 19 kilometres remaining. French prodigy Paul Seixas, a 20-year-old competing for Decathlon–CMA CGM, launched a ferocious solo attack. Within a kilometre, Seixas dropped key contenders, including Evenepoel, pre-race favourite Isaac del Toro, and world champion Brandon McNulty, before riding solo for over 18 kilometres.","Seixas crossed the finish line 48 seconds ahead of Spain's Enric Mas, while Giulio Ciccone finished third. In winning his maiden Monument title in just his third appearance at this level, Seixas became the youngest champion in Giro di Lombardia history and the youngest Monument winner since 1944. He also became the first Frenchman to win a Monument since Julian Alaphilippe in 2019 and the first to win Lombardia since Thibaut Pinot in 2018.","This triumph capped a remarkable debut season for Seixas, who had already placed fourth at the Tour de France, won La Flèche Wallonne, and finished second at Liège–Bastogne–Liège. Although he suffered late cramping at the recent World Championships, his brilliant ride cemented his rise to cycling stardom. Nevertheless, the riders' pre-race silence lingered, delivering an undeniable message that athlete safety must remain non-negotiable."],"facts":[{"fact":"Riders at the Giro di Lombardia held a five-minute silent protest before starting the race from Bergamo to Como to protest safety concerns and vehicle collisions.","sources":[1,2]},{"fact":"Remco Evenepoel addressed the crowd during the protest, citing recent vehicle incidents including the death of 19-year-old British rider Finlay Tarling at the Tour of Portugal in August.","sources":[1,2]},{"fact":"Earlier that week at the Tre Valli Varesine, Thomas Gloag and Marcel Camprubi were struck by a police motorcycle travelling in the opposite direction.","sources":[1,2]},{"fact":"The CPA riders' union demanded that the UCI and organizers take responsibility and enhance safety protocols.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Tadej Pogacar, who won the previous five editions of the race, missed the event due to a season-ending injury sustained at the Vuelta a Espana.","sources":[1,2]},{"fact":"Twenty-year-old French rider Paul Seixas broke away with 19km remaining to win the race, becoming the youngest winner of Giro di Lombardia and the youngest Monument winner since 1944.","sources":[1,2]},{"fact":"Enric Mas finished second, 48 seconds behind Seixas, and Giulio Ciccone finished third.","sources":[1,2]}],"glossary":[{"text":"autumn","lemma":"autumn","pos":"noun","he":"סתיו","name":false},{"text":"classic","lemma":"classic","pos":"noun","he":"מרוץ קלאסי / קלאסיקה","name":false},{"text":"known as","lemma":"know as","pos":"verb","he":"מוכר כ-","name":false},{"text":"Giro di Lombardia","lemma":"Giro di Lombardia","pos":"noun","he":"ג'ירו די לומברדיה","name":true},{"text":"traditionally","lemma":"traditionally","pos":"adverb","he":"כמסורת / לפי המסורת","name":false},{"text":"dubbed","lemma":"dub","pos":"verb","he":"כונה /